	<item>
		<title>By: Raphaela Wendy Zieve</title>
		<link>http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=647&#038;cpage=1#comment-10690</link>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Raphaela Wendy Zieve]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Wed, 30 Nov 2011 16:29:10 +0000</pubDate>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=647#comment-10690</guid>
		<description><![CDATA[I had my iPOD with lots of old jazz tunes on it.  For fun,  I sat with Shakur to play &quot;Name That Tune.&quot;   Not only could he name each and every tune within 2 to 4 measures,  but he could tell me when and where it was recorded,  who was playing trombone,  trumpet, etc.  and also had many stories of having contacted these musicians personally and some of them gracing him with free tickets,  records and conversation.
He had been a radio DJ and owned hundreds of records.]]></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>I had my iPOD with lots of old jazz tunes on it.  For fun,  I sat with Shakur to play &#8220;Name That Tune.&#8221;   Not only could he name each and every tune within 2 to 4 measures,  but he could tell me when and where it was recorded,  who was playing trombone,  trumpet, etc.  and also had many stories of having contacted these musicians personally and some of them gracing him with free tickets,  records and conversation.<br />
He had been a radio DJ and owned hundreds of records.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
	</item>
	<item>
		<title>By: Tim Jackson</title>
		<link>http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=647&#038;cpage=1#comment-3062</link>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Tim Jackson]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Wed, 25 Nov 2009 23:45:16 +0000</pubDate>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=647#comment-3062</guid>
		<description><![CDATA[Shukur was a mureed of Wali Ali&#039;s, probably initiated around the early
90&#039;s.  He was a regular attendee of the Northwest Sufi Camp and other
Sufi activities in the Pacific Northwest from the late 70&#039;s on.  Physically
he suffered from a debilitating disease that confined him to a wheel
chair and took his vision but his wit and joy for music never left him.
He was a consummate punster and story teller.  Loved jazz and old
radio and hugs.]]></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Shukur was a mureed of Wali Ali&#8217;s, probably initiated around the early<br />
90&#8217;s.  He was a regular attendee of the Northwest Sufi Camp and other<br />
Sufi activities in the Pacific Northwest from the late 70&#8217;s on.  Physically<br />
he suffered from a debilitating disease that confined him to a wheel<br />
chair and took his vision but his wit and joy for music never left him.<br />
He was a consummate punster and story teller.  Loved jazz and old<br />
radio and hugs.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
